 ### [Feed-forward and Generalizable Prediction](15832-feed-forward-and-generalizable-prediction.md)
-Single forward pass for generalizable reconstruction.
+Feed-forward and generalizable prediction methods for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) replace the iterative, per-scene optimization of standard 3DGS with a single forward pass of a neural network to predict Gaussian parameters directly from input images. This approach solves the problem of slow reconstruction and the dependency on accurate camera poses (typically obtained via Structure-from-Motion) by leveraging pretrained foundation models for geometry and appearance. Unlike inverse methods that optimize a scene representation for a specific set of views, these models generalize across scenes, enabling novel view synthesis and 3D reconstruction from uncalibrated, unordered, or sparse image sets without fine-tuning.
 
 ### BrightDreamer
-Predicts the deviation of a set of fixed anchor points to determine the centers of Gaussians [S1].
+BrightDreamer predicts the deviation of a set of fixed anchor points to determine the centers of Gaussians [S1]. The centers of Gaussians are determined from these deviations [S1].
 
 ### Defocus Blur Modeling
-Models optical defocus by adjusting the scale $s$ of 3D Gaussians based on their distance from the camera's focal plane [S6]. Larger $s$ models out-of-focus points, smaller $s$ models in-focus points [S6].
+Defocus Blur Modeling adjusts the scale $s$ of 3D Gaussians based on their distance from the camera's focal plane [S6]. During training, larger scale values $\bar{s}_j$ are used to rasterize blurred images, while smaller scale values $s_j$ are used during testing to render sharp details [S6]. The scale $s$ controls dispersion; larger $s$ models out-of-focus points, smaller $s$ models in-focus points [S6]. Unlike a naive Gaussian kernel that blurs the entire image uniformly, this method selectively enlarges covariances only for Gaussians corresponding to blurred regions [S6].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward methods (e.g., EVolSplat, GRM) are significantly faster than iterative optimization, enabling real-time rendering [S5].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Global volume methods require storing a 3D feature volume, which can be large for urban scenes [S5]. Sequential decoders reduce memory by specializing decoders [S2].
-*   **Performance:** EVolSplat achieves real-time rendering for urban scenes [S5]. GRM enables fast 3D-aware generation [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time inference, generalizability across scenes, reduced ghosting artifacts compared to pixel-aligned methods [S5].
-*   **Limitations:** Dependency on depth estimator quality [S5], potential loss of high-frequency details if primitives are downsampled [S5].
+- **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward prediction is significantly faster than iterative optimization, enabling real-time rendering [S5].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the feature volume size and the number of Gaussians. Global volume methods may require more memory than local cost volume methods but reduce redundancy [S5].
+- **Performance:** EVolSplat achieves real-time rendering and reduces ghosting artifacts compared to pixel-aligned methods [S5].
+- **Strengths:** Fast, global consistency, handles urban scenes well [S5].
+- **Limitations:** Requires accurate depth estimation; noisy depth can lead to artifacts if not filtered [S5].
 
 ## Background
-Originated from the need to accelerate 3D Gaussian Splatting reconstruction, which traditionally requires slow per-scene optimization [S5]. EVolSplat was introduced in 2025 for efficient urban view synthesis [S5]. GRM and sequential decoding approaches were developed for 3D-aware generative models [S2]. BrightDreamer predicts anchor deviations for 3D generation [S1].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction originated with methods like EVolSplat, which introduced a feed-forward 3DGS model for urban view synthesis using a global volumetric approach [S5]. It builds on the foundation of 3D Gaussian Splatting [15] and addresses limitations of per-scene optimization [S5].
 
